"On minimum-size matroid counterexamples to the multiplicative Merino--Welsh inequality (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27706)\nciteturn1academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; The multiplicative Merino--Welsh inequality \\[ \\Tutte_M(2,0)\\Tutte_M(0,2)\\geq \\Tutte_M(1,1)^2 \\] was proved to be false for matroids by Beke, Csáji, Csikvári, and Pituk (\\emph{Adv. ... For matroids, we further prove that every element of a minimum-size loopless and coloopless counterexample lies in exactly one series or parallel class of size two.\nTitle: On minimum-size matroid counterexamples to the multiplicative Merino--Welsh inequality\nAuthors: Jungang Chen, Xian'an Jin, Tianlong Ma\nDate: Tue Oct 20 06:23:41 2026\n\nLet $M$ be a matroid and let $\\Tutte_M(x,y)$ be its Tutte polynomial. The multiplicative Merino--Welsh inequality \\[ \\Tutte_M(2,0)\\Tutte_M(0,2)\\geq \\Tutte_M(1,1)^2 \\] was proved to be false for matroids by Beke, Csáji, Csikvári, and Pituk (\\emph{Adv. Math.} \\textbf{446} (2024), 109674). Let $U_{n,r}$ denote the rank-$r$ uniform matroid on $n$ elements, where $0<r<n$, and let $U_{n,r}^{(q)}$ denote the matroid obtained by replacing each element of $U_{n,r}$ with $q$ parallel copies. They constructed an infinite family of counterexamples of the form $U_{n,r}^{(2)}$, including $U_{33,22}^{(2)}$. In this paper, we prove that every $U_{n,r}^{(q)}$ with $q\\geq3$ satisfies the inequality. For $q=2$, we prove that the minimum number of elements in such a counterexample is $62$, attained by $U_{31,21}^{(2)}$. For loopless and bridgeless graphs, Noble and Royle (\\emph{Eur. J. Comb.} \\textbf{38} (2014), 24--35) showed that every edge of a minimum-size counterexample to the multiplicative Merino--Welsh conjecture lies in a series or parallel class of size two or three. For matroids, we further prove that every element of a minimum-size loopless and coloopless counterexample lies in exactly one series or parallel class of size two.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn minimum-size matroid counterexamples to the multiplicative Merino--Welsh inequality (https://borowiecki.dev/abs/2610.27706)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; The multiplicative Merino--Welsh inequality \\[ \\Tutte_M(2,0)\\Tutte_M(0,2)\\geq \\Tutte_M(1,1)^2 \\] was proved to be false for matroids by Beke, Csáji, Csikvári, and Pituk (\\emph{Adv. ... For matroids, we further prove that every element of a minimum-size loopless and coloopless counterexample lies in exactly one series or parallel class of size two.\n\n# Mathematics > Combinatorics\n\n[Submitted on 20 Oct 2026]\n\n# Title:On minimum-size matroid counterexamples to the multiplicative Merino--Welsh inequality\n\nAuthors:Jungang Chen, Xian'an Jin, Tianlong Ma\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled On minimum-size matroid counterexamples to the multiplicative Merino--Welsh inequality, by Jungang Chen and Xian'an Jin and Tianlong Ma\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:Let $M$ be a matroid and let $\\Tutte_M(x,y)$ be its Tutte polynomial. The multiplicative Merino--Welsh inequality \\[ \\Tutte_M(2,0)\\Tutte_M(0,2)\\geq \\Tutte_M(1,1)^2 \\] was proved to be false for matroids by Beke, Csáji, Csikvári, and Pituk (\\emph{Adv. Math.} \\textbf{446} (2024), 109674). Let $U_{n,r}$ denote the rank-$r$ uniform matroid on $n$ elements, where $0<r<n$, and let $U_{n,r}^{(q)}$ denote the matroid obtained by replacing each element of $U_{n,r}$ with $q$ parallel copies. They constructed an infinite family of counterexamples of the form $U_{n,r}^{(2)}$, including $U_{33,22}^{(2)}$. In this paper, we prove that every $U_{n,r}^{(q)}$ with $q\\geq3$ satisfies the inequality. For $q=2$, we prove that the minimum number of elements in such a counterexample is $62$, attained by $U_{31,21}^{(2)}$. For loopless and bridgeless graphs, Noble and Royle (\\emph{Eur. J. Comb.} \\textbf{38} (2014), 24--35) showed that every edge of a minimum-size counterexample to the multiplicative Merino--Welsh conjecture lies in a series or parallel class of size two or three. For matroids, we further prove that every element of a minimum-size loopless and coloopless counterexample lies in exactly one series or parallel class of size two.\n\nComments:  | 19 pages\nSubjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO)\nMSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 05A20, 05B35, 05C31\nCite as:  | arXiv:2610.27706 [math.CO]\n   | (or arXiv:2610.27706v1 [math.CO] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.27706 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite (pending registration)\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Jungang Chen [view email]\n[v1] Tue, 20 Oct 2026 06:23:41 UTC (14 KB)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAll works · Hexagon (https://hexagonmath.org/search?cursor=eyJ2IjoxLCJzb3J0RGF0ZSI6IjIwMjYtMTAtMjFUMTQ6MjI6MTEuMjQyWiIsImlkZW50aWZpZXIiOiIyNjEwLjAwNDM4In0&direction=next&page=3&per_page=100)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; In particular, this disproves **Conjecture 7.2 of Csikvári (2026)**, which asserts the multiplicative Merino–Welsh inequality for simple and cosimple matroids, and therefore also the stronger **Conjecture 7.3** therein, as observed by its author. ... Taking the direct sum of a counterexample with its dual also gives a negative answer to **Problem 4.3 of Beke, Csáji, Csikvári, and Pituk (2024)**: the original maximum and additive Merino–Welsh inequalities can fail for simple and cosimple matroids.\n\nContributed by Jungang Chen\n\nFor every integer $k\\ge 3$k≥3, we construct $k$k-connected matroids representable over every infinite field, with girth $k$k, arbitrarily large cogirth, and Merino–Welsh quotient \\[ \\Phi(M)=\\frac{T_M(1,1)^2}{T_M(2,0)T_M(0,2)} \\] tending to infinity. In particular, this disproves **Conjecture 7.2 of Csikvári (2026)**, which asserts the multiplicative Merino–Welsh inequality for simple and cosimple matroids, and therefore also the stronger **Conjecture 7.3** therein, as observed by its author. Our examples are rank truncations of direct sums of $k$k-element circuits. We give a 3-connected counterexample on 1011 elements of rank 433, certified by exact integer arithmetic, and a 6048-element counterexample admitting a proof without computation. Taking the direct sum of a counterexample with its dual also gives a negative answer to **Problem 4.3 of Beke, Csáji, Csikvári, and Pituk (2024)**: the original maximum and additive Merino–Welsh inequalities can fail for simple and cosimple matroids. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMerino-Welsh Conjecture (https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Merino-WelshConjecture.html)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Chen et al. (2026) determined the smallest multiplicative counterexample among parallel thickenings of uniform matroids of matroid rank Image: r on Image: n elements with Image: 0<r<n. ... \"The Sharp Threshold for the Multiplicative Merino-Welsh Inequality on Matroids.\"\n\n# Merino-Welsh Conjecture\n\nThe Merino-Welsh conjecture (Merino and Welsh 1999) asserts that every connected graph Image: G without graph loops or graph bridges satisfies\n\nImage: max{T_G(2,0),T_G(0,2)}>=T_G(1,1).   | (1)\n\nHere Image: T_G is the Tutte polynomial. The three quantities count graph orientations having no directed cycles, graph orientations in which every edge lies on a directed cycle, and spanning trees, respectively.\n\nThe multiplicative Merino-Welsh conjecture (Conde and Merino 2009) is the stronger inequality\n\nImage: T_G(2,0)T_G(0,2)>=T_G(1,1)^2.   | (2)\n\nIt remains open for graphs. Its direct extension to matroids without loops or coloops was refuted by Beke et al. (2024). A matroid loop belongs to no matroid basis, while a coloop belongs to every matroid basis.\n\nCsikvári (2026) conjectured a sharp universal replacement for matroids. Let Image: x_*=2.226681596905677... be the largest real polynomial root of\n\nImage: x^3=9(x-1).   | (3)\n\nLiu (2026) reported a proof that every finite matroid Image: M of this type satisfies\n\nImage: T_M(x_*,0)T_M(0,x_*)>=T_M(1,1)^2.   | (4)\n\nKnown counterexamples for every Image: 0<=x<x_* make Image: x_* the least nonnegative universal parameter. More precisely, if Image: M has Image: m elements and Image: kappa connected components, then for Image: 2<=x<=x_*,\n\nImage: (T_M(x,0)T_M(0,x))/(T_M(1,1)^2)>=((x^3)/(9(x-1)))^((m-2kappa)/2).   | (5)\n\nExamples in Liu (2026) show that the exponential rate is sharp throughout this interval.\n\nLiu (2026) reports that GPT-6 Astra and Claude Opus 5 assisted with mathematical exploration, proof development, exact computation, and code. As of Sep. 27, 2026, the result had not undergone external peer review, and the rational replacement table used by the proof had not been independently recomputed.\n\nChen and Xie (2026) gave a separate direct inductive proof of the universal threshold inequality Image: T_M(x_*,0)T_M(0,x_*)>=T_M(1,1)^2 for loopless and coloopless matroids. This is the same threshold already covered by Liu (2026), not a proof of the original multiplicative conjecture for graphs.\n\nFor chordal graphs, the original multiplicative inequality has been proved. Xu et al. (2026) showed that every connected bridgeless simple chordal graph satisfies Image: T_G(2,0)T_G(0,2)>=T_G(1,1)^2. Xu et al. (2026) report using ChatGPT to explore possible approaches to the proof.\n\nChen et al. (2026) determined the smallest multiplicative counterexample among parallel thickenings of uniform matroids of matroid rank Image: r on Image: n elements with Image: 0<r<n. It has 62 elements and is obtained by replacing each element of the rank-21 uniform matroid on 31 elements by two parallel copies. Every Image: q-thickening in this family with Image: q>=3 satisfies the inequality. The 62-element minimum is restricted to this family and is not asserted over all matroids or over graphs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMinimum Maximal Matchings in Cubic Graphs (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v29i2p36/pdf)\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.5 years ago; On the other hand, it is well known that every maximal matching of G provides a ... factor strictly smaller than 2, see e.g. [6, 7, 19, 28], yet most of the works just mentioned ... In this paper, we improve these results by proving Conjecture 1 for cubic graphs: ... Let G be a connected subcubic graph on n vertices and m edges. ... not cubic then γ(G) ⩽4n−m\nconditions that the graph is bipartite and does not contain a certain 6-vertex tree as an\ninduced subgraph.\nThe\n9n\n20 + O(1) bound for cubic graphs was previously derived by\nDuckworth and Wormald through a linear programming analysis of greedy algorithms on\ncubic graphs [15]. With a more problem-speciﬁc approach, Zwo´zniak [30] obtained the\nbetter bound γ(G) ⩽4n\n9 + 1\n3.\nIn this paper, we improve these results by proving Conjecture 1 for cubic graphs:\nTheorem 2. Let G be a connected cubic graph of order n. Then\nγ(G) ⩽5\n12n + 1\n2,\nwith equality if and only if G is isomorphic to the complete bipartite graph K3,3.\nIn doing so, we derive the following slightly more general result, which also applies to\nnon-regular graphs.\nTheorem 3. Let G be a connected subcubic graph on n vertices and m edges. Then\nγ(G) ⩽4n−m\n6\n+ 1\n2, with equality if and only if G is isomorphic to K3,3. Moreover, if G is\nnot cubic then γ(G) ⩽4n−m\n6\n.\nAn eﬀective and widely used approach to prove theorems on subcubic graphs is to\ntake a hypothetical minimum counterexample and then analyse small cut sets as well as\nthe local structure around vertices of small degree, followed by an analysis of the local\nstructure in a highly connected cubic graph, eventually leading to a contradiction (see\ne.g. [4, 3, 21] for some recent examples). This paper is no exception. However, one way\nin which perhaps our approach stands out is how we carefully almost avoid the need to\nanalyse any cubic graph (at the cost of a slightly worse result than is perhaps possible;\nsee the discussion of Conjecture 7). This is desirable because the cubic graphs form the\nhard cases in our context. Relatedly, to make the ‘induction’ work, we have devised the\nfollowing technical theorem, which has Theorems 2 and 3 as direct corollaries.\nthe electronic journal of combinatorics 29(2) (2022), #P2.36\n2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the other hand, it is well known that every maximal matching of \\(G\\) provides a 2-approximation for \\(\\gamma(G)\\). There exist efficient algorithms that approach \\(\\gamma(G)\\) within a factor strictly smaller than 2, see e.g. [6, 7, 19, 28], yet most of the works just mentioned focus on the general case or on very dense graphs. There also exist polynomial-time algorithms that compute \\(\\gamma(G)\\) for more restricted graph classes, e.g. trees [25], series-parallel graphs [27] and graphs of bounded clique width [16]. In this paper, we instead focus on graphs with bounded degree. Concretely, we study the following recent conjecture of Baste, Fürst, Henning, Mohr and Rautenbach. (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v29i2p36/pdf/)\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.5 years ago; On the other hand, it is well known that every maximal matching of \\(G\\) provides a 2-approximation for \\(\\gamma(G)\\). ... ** *Let \\(G\\) be a connected subcubic graph on \\(n\\) vertices and \\(m\\) edges. ... Moreover, if \\(G\\) is not cubic then \\(\\gamma(G)\\le \\frac{4n-m}{6}\\).\nFurthermore, for cubic graphs they confirmed Conjecture 1 under the additional conditions that the graph is bipartite and does not contain a certain 6-vertex tree as an induced subgraph. The \\(\\frac{9n}{20}+O(1)\\) bound for cubic graphs was previously derived by Duckworth and Wormald through a linear programming analysis of greedy algorithms on cubic graphs [15]. With a more problem-specific approach, Zwoźniak [30] obtained the better bound \\(\\gamma(G)\\le \\frac{4n}{9}+\\frac{1}{3}\\).\n\nIn this paper, we improve these results by proving Conjecture 1 for cubic graphs:\n\n**Theorem 2.** *Let \\(G\\) be a connected cubic graph of order \\(n\\). Then*\n\n\\[\n\\gamma(G)\\le \\frac{5}{12}n+\\frac{1}{2},\n\\]\n\n*with equality if and only if \\(G\\) is isomorphic to the complete bipartite graph \\(K_{3,3}\\).*\n\nIn doing so, we derive the following slightly more general result, which also applies to non-regular graphs.\n\n**Theorem 3.** *Let \\(G\\) be a connected subcubic graph on \\(n\\) vertices and \\(m\\) edges. Then*\n\\[\n\\gamma(G)\\le \\frac{4n-m}{6}+\\frac{1}{2},\n\\]\n*with equality if and only if \\(G\\) is isomorphic to \\(K_{3,3}\\). Moreover, if \\(G\\) is not cubic then \\(\\gamma(G)\\le \\frac{4n-m}{6}\\).*\n\nAn effective and widely used approach to prove theorems on subcubic graphs is to take a hypothetical minimum counterexample and then analyse small cut sets as well as the local structure around vertices of small degree, followed by an analysis of the local structure in a highly connected cubic graph, eventually leading to a contradiction (see e.g. [4, 3, 21] for some recent examples). This paper is no exception. However, one way in which perhaps our approach stands out is how we carefully almost avoid the need to analyse any cubic graph (at the cost of a slightly worse result than is perhaps possible; see the discussion of Conjecture 7). This is desirable because the cubic graphs form the hard cases in our context. Relatedly, to make the ‘induction’ work, we have devised the following technical theorem, which has Theorems 2 and 3 as direct corollaries.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPapers from 2026-10-06 - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/date/2026-10-06)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: today; A direct inductive proof of the sharp Merino--Welsh threshold for matroids\n\nMotivated by the Merino--Welsh conjecture, we consider the smallest $c\\ge0$, denoted by $c_*$, for which the inequality $T_M(c,0)T_M(0,c)\\ge T_M(1,1)^2$ holds for every loopless and coloopless finite matroid $M$.\n\n2026-10-06 v2\n\nJungang Chen, Jiaxin Xie\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nA direct inductive proof of the sharp Merino--Welsh threshold for matroids\n\nMotivated by the Merino--Welsh conjecture, we consider the smallest $c\\ge0$, denoted by $c_*$, for which the inequality $T_M(c,0)T_M(0,c)\\ge T_M(1,1)^2$ holds for every loopless and coloopless finite matroid $M$. The counterexamples constructed by Beke, Csáji, Csikvári, and Pituk [\\emph{Adv. Math.} \\textbf{446} (2024), 109674] give the lower bound $x_0$, where $x_0\\approx2.22668$ is the largest real root of the polynomial $x^3-9(x-1)$. Later, Csikvári [\\emph{European J. Combin.} \\textbf{137} (2026), 104402] improved the known upper bound for this constant to $2.35$ and then conjectured that the above inequality holds at $c=x_0$. This conjecture was recently proved by Liu (2026). We give an alternative direct inductive proof that $c_*=x_0$, without computer-assisted finite verification.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Merino-Welsh conjecture is false for matroids · ProbXiv (https://probxiv.com/problems/the-merino-welsh-conjecture-is-false-for-matroids-2)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # The Merino-Welsh conjecture is false for matroids ... This is the standard maximum form used in the cited paper; the additive and multiplicative variants are stronger. ... I found no published binary counterexample; known counterexamples use non-binary uniform matroid thickenings.\n\n$$T_Q(2,0)=T_Q(0,2)=T_P(2,0)T_P(0,2)<T_P(1,1)^2=T_Q(1,1).$$T Q​(2,0)=T Q​(0,2)=T P​(2,0)T P​(0,2)<T P​(1,1)2=T Q​(1,1).\n\nThus\n\n$$\\max\\{T_Q(2,0),T_Q(0,2)\\}<T_Q(1,1),$$max{T Q​(2,0),T Q​(0,2)}<T Q​(1,1),\n\nso the Merino–Welsh conjecture is false for binary matroids.\n\nAudit: no simplicity or connectedness assumption was used; the reconstructed statement only requires finite binary matroids without loops and coloops. The constructed $Q$Q satisfies exactly those hypotheses.\n\nCitation: The formulation is from Beke–Csáji–Csikvári–Pituk, “The Merino–Welsh conjecture is false for matroids,” arXiv:2311.01932. The binary counterexample above is proved here; no external binary counterexample is invoked.\n\n  2. Read by a language model on #1 · not a proof\n\nmodel says: correct GPT-5.5 xhigh (SMD judge 1)Aug 17, 2026\n\nscope Full solution as submitted; SMD novelty classification TYPE2\n\n[Input]\n\nPASS\n\nThe proof correctly attacks the loopless/coloopless binary-matroid version. The random binary matroid step gives, for large $n$n, a rank $r=2n/3$r=2 n/3 binary matroid with more than $2^{5n/6}$2 5 n/6 bases. The 2-thickening remains binary and has no coloops, multiplies the number of bases by $2^r$2 r, and preserves $T(2,0)$T(2,0). The bounds\n\n$$T_P(2,0)\\le 2^n,\\qquad T_P(0,2)\\le 2^{2n}$$T P​(2,0)≤2 n,T P​(0,2)≤2 2 n\n\nare valid using standard Tutte evaluations and nonnegative coefficients. Hence $P$P violates the multiplicative inequality, and $Q=P\\oplus P^*$Q=P⊕P∗ is binary, loopless, coloopless, and violates the maximum Merino–Welsh inequality.\n\nI found no published binary counterexample; known counterexamples use non-binary uniform matroid thickenings.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[Literature Review] On minimum-size matroid counterexamples to the multiplicative Merino--Welsh inequality (https://www.themoonlight.io/en/review/on-minimum-size-matroid-counterexamples-to-the-multiplicative-merin